          <p>
            Add the extras people forget: the garage apron, the front walk, the steps and the area around the mailbox. Those often add 150 to 300 square feet. Buying a little extra is cheaper than stopping halfway through with a dry strip that will show up as a dark band in the next rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrates change the math. A product sold as a concentrate makes several gallons of finished sealer per jug, so compare the cost per finished gallon, not per container. At the time of writing, penetrating sealer material for a two-car driveway commonly runs from roughly one hundred to several hundred dollars depending on product and coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film sealers spread further on the second coat because the first has filled the pores. Many list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon for coat one and more for coat two. Do not stretch them thinner than the label to save money; thin spots wear through first in tire paths.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Yearly Upkeep Between Sealings
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealed driveway still needs a little care each year. In spring, sweep and rinse off winter grit and salt residue. Check joints and cracks; caulk-type joint sealant shrinks and pulls away over time, and an open joint lets water into the base, which leads to heaving and settling. A self-leveling polyurethane joint sealant is the usual fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle oil promptly. Penetrating repellents slow oil penetration but do not stop it, so blot fresh drips with absorbent and use a concrete degreaser on spots before they set. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers the methods that work on older stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a water drop test each fall. Pour a little water on the busiest areas, such as the tire paths, apron and parking spot. If the water soaks in and darkens the concrete within a minute or two, that area needs resealing before winter. Often only the high-wear zones need a fresh coat, which saves time and material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the edges protected. Driveway edges crack more easily because they are thinner and unsupported. Keep downspouts aimed away from the slab, fill soil along the edges so they are not undercut, and avoid driving off the edge onto the lawn. Small habits like these do more for a driveway's lifespan than any single coat of sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

