        <div id="how-driveways-fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. How Concrete Driveways Actually Fail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most driveway damage happens in the top quarter inch. That layer, the paste and fine sand troweled up during finishing, is the weakest part of the slab. When water soaks into it and freezes, it expands roughly 9 percent. Do that dozens of times a winter and the surface starts to shed thin flakes, a failure called scaling. Spalling is the bigger version, where larger chunks pop off, often over a corroding rebar or a weak spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt makes it worse in two ways. Salt solutions soak deeper into concrete than plain water, and they keep the surface wet longer, which means more freeze-thaw cycles in the saturated zone. Chlorides that reach reinforcing steel also start corrosion, and rust occupies more volume than the steel it replaces, cracking the concrete from inside. That is why bridge decks and parking garages are routinely treated with silane-based repellents.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tires and vehicles add mechanical wear. Turning wheels on a stationary car grind the surface, heavy trucks flex thin edges, and plows and shovels chip whatever is on top. A driveway sealer has to survive all of that, which is the main reason film-forming sealers wear out so much faster on driveways than on patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            What sealing cannot fix is just as important. Structural cracks from settling, heaving from frost in a poorly drained base, and existing scaling are not reversed by any sealer. A sealer slows future damage on sound concrete. If your driveway already has wide cracks or sunken panels, sort those out first; our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">driveway crack filler guide</a> covers the products for joints and hairline cracks.
          </p>
        </div>

