            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Add the extras people forget: the garage apron, the front walk, the steps and the area around the mailbox. Those often add 150 to 300 square feet. Buying a little extra is cheaper than stopping halfway through with a dry strip that will show up as a dark band in the next rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrates change the math. A product sold as a concentrate makes several gallons of finished sealer per jug, so compare the cost per finished gallon, not per container. At the time of writing, penetrating sealer material for a two-car driveway commonly runs from roughly one hundred to several hundred dollars depending on product and coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film sealers spread further on the second coat because the first has filled the pores. Many list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon for coat one and more for coat two. Do not stretch them thinner than the label to save money; thin spots wear through first in tire paths.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            NaN. Yearly Upkeep Between Sealings
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sealed driveway still needs a little care each year. In spring, sweep and rinse off winter grit and salt residue. Check joints and cracks; caulk-type joint sealant shrinks and pulls away over time, and an open joint lets water into the base, which leads to heaving and settling. A self-leveling polyurethane joint sealant is the usual fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle oil promptly. Penetrating repellents slow oil penetration but do not stop it, so blot fresh drips with absorbent and use a concrete degreaser on spots before they set. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers the methods that work on older stains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a water drop test each fall. Pour a little water on the busiest areas, such as the tire paths, apron and parking spot. If the water soaks in and darkens the concrete within a minute or two, that area needs resealing before winter. Often only the high-wear zones need a fresh coat, which saves time and material.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the edges protected. Driveway edges crack more easily because they are thinner and unsupported. Keep downspouts aimed away from the slab, fill soil along the edges so they are not undercut, and avoid driving off the edge onto the lawn. Small habits like these do more for a driveway's lifespan than any single coat of sealer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing too late in the year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Waiting until after the first salting means the slab enters winter unprotected, and a cold, damp fall can make a proper application impossible. Seal in late summer or early fall when temperatures are reliably within the label range and the concrete can dry out completely before the sealer goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a glossy film on a plain driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wet-look acrylic on a sloped, broom-finished driveway trades long-term protection and traction for appearance. It wears in tire paths within a few seasons and gets slick in rain and frost. Unless the concrete is decorative, a penetrating sealer will protect it longer and cost less over time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring open joints and cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing the surface while control joints sit open lets water run straight into the base. That leads to heaving and sunken panels, which no sealer can fix. Fill joints and cracks with a flexible polyurethane sealant before sealing, and recheck them every spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading ammonium-based ice melt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deicers containing ammonium nitrate or ammonium sulfate attack concrete chemically and can cause severe scaling, even on a sealed slab. Read the label before buying any ice melt, especially fertilizer-style products, and choose calcium or magnesium chloride used sparingly instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on it too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh sealer, especially a film, can mark or lift under tires before it cures. Most penetrating sealers want around a day before vehicles, while film sealers often need two or three. Block the driveway off with cones or tape and warn delivery drivers if needed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which sealer type suits most concrete driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most driveways, a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer is the best choice. It keeps water and road salt out of the surface, does not change the look or traction, and has nothing on top to wear under tires. If you salt heavily, choose one formulated for chloride resistance. Use a film sealer only when you want a wet look or strong color enhancement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a concrete driveway be resealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating sealers are commonly rated for five to ten years, with heavily salted, high-traffic driveways at the low end. Film-forming acrylics usually need recoating every two to four years. Rather than following a calendar, do a water drop test each fall and reseal any area where water soaks in quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing stop my driveway from cracking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It does not prevent structural cracks, which come from settling, frost heave, tree roots and heavy loads. Sealing reduces surface damage such as scaling and spalling by keeping water and salt out of the top layer, and it can slow the growth of small cracks by limiting freeze-thaw action. Fill cracks before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal a new concrete driveway before winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, if you choose the right product. Many penetrating silane-siloxane sealers allow application earlier than the 28 days typical for acrylics, sometimes after a week or two. Check the label. Getting protection on before the first deicer is especially valuable, because new concrete is most vulnerable during its first winter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a sealed driveway be slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A penetrating sealer will not change traction because it leaves no film. Film sealers, especially high-gloss wet-look products, can be slick when wet or frosty. If you use a film on a driveway or walk, mix an anti-slip grit additive into the final coat and keep coats thin.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth hiring a pro to seal a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical driveway, DIY sealing with a pump sprayer is very manageable and saves most of the cost. A pro is worth considering if the slab needs significant crack repair, densifying and repellent work, stripping of an old film, or if you lack time for proper cleaning and drying. Ask any contractor which product and how many coats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use asphalt sealer on a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt sealcoat is designed for flexible asphalt pavement and will look blotchy, wear off quickly and can be difficult to remove from concrete. Concrete needs a penetrating repellent, a silicate densifier or a concrete-rated acrylic. For blacktop, see our asphalt driveway sealer reviews.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

